[#:seon.task{:name
            "edit-hook-refuses-a-lawful-throw-of-a-registered-error",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "`seon.contracts-compile-test/uncontained-changes` (run by the PreToolUse edit hook bin/seon-hook) refuses ANY edit to a defn that throws ex-info carrying a registered `:seon.error/type` with error/uncontained-throw unless the output union declares an error alternative. CLAUDE.md and docs/seon/specs/errors.md say the opposite: a function whose job is not to answer throws the one error map inside ex-info and declares only its success value. Reproduce: edit any line of `seon.id/structural-digest` (it throws `:seon.id/unhashable-error`, registered in seon.id.edn): the hook answers 'seon.id/structural-digest (row 127) throws to its caller 1 way(s) with no error alternative'. This blocked an EQUIVALENT 9-line speedup of seon.id (patch in docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/reuse-audit/reuse-audit-notes.md). Correct: a throw whose ex-data is a registered :seon.error variant built by the installed constructor or a declared literal is lawful and not a finding; keep refusing throws of undeclared data. Proof: a contracts-compile-test case where a changed defn throwing a registered variant yields no :uncontained-throw finding.",
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/reuse-audit/reuse-audit-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "empty-input-calls-analyzer-with-a-refused-empty-source-map",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "`seon.contracts-compile-test/check` with :paths [] reaches namespace-contexts and calls `seon.fn.analyzer/analyze` with an empty sources map; the analyzer requires min 1 and throws a contract refusal. Return zero contract findings without analyzing when the selected source set is empty; preserve placement findings. Reproduced on default 42e569d9d, JVM root, result/e0f9f608c27e4. Regression should invoke check with no selected sources and assert its data result. Evidence: docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/spec-reviews/review-final-codex-notes.md.",
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/spec-reviews/review-final-codex-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "corpus-check-allocation-is-floored-by-edamame-per-char-boxing",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "`seon.contracts-compile-test/check` over the whole corpus (472 src/test files, 10.0 M chars) allocates about 1.7 GB after the contract-check lane's read-once fix; about 1,040 MB of it is one edamame parse (`seon.contracts-compile-test/read-source`, ~100 bytes per char). JFR allocation samples: edamame.impl.parser/read-token and parse-string* call (.append sb ch) with a boxed Character, which resolves to StringBuilder.append(Object) and allocates a String per character (~31% of edamame allocation); location-metadata assoc/vary-meta ~20%. Probe in a throwaway JVM: patching the three appends to (.append sb (char ch)) cut a corpus parse from 1,042 MB to 832 MB. reference-code/edamame is upstream borkdude at 196ea5e4ef, not a fork: the fix is an upstream patch (or a deliberate fork decision by the owner). The remaining ~400 MB is clj-kondo over 471 ns prefixes (`seon.fn.analyzer/invoke-kondo`, ~0.85 MB per file). Done when a whole-corpus check allocates under 1 GB, measured with getThreadAllocatedBytes.",
            :evidence
            #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/contract-check/contract-check-notes.md"}}]
